============================================================
1. NEW PROJECT CHECKLIST
============================================================

Follow these steps every time you start a new Python project.

STEP 1: OPEN THE TERMINAL

Open your terminal application.

macOS:
    Terminal app or iTerm

Windows:
    Windows Terminal, Command Prompt, or PowerShell

STEP 2: NAVIGATE TO YOUR PROJECTS FOLDER

Go to your projects directory.

macOS:

    cd ~/Documents/Projects

Windows PowerShell:

    cd "$HOME\Documents\Projects"

If the Projects folder does not exist, create it first.

macOS/Linux:

    mkdir -p ~/Documents/Projects

Windows PowerShell:

    New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force "$HOME\Documents\Projects"

TIP:

Create a dedicated folder for all your Python projects. This keeps your work organized and makes it easier to find your projects later.

STEP 3: CREATE THE PROJECT

Use uv to create your project:

    uv init my-awesome-project

This creates a new folder named my-awesome-project.

It also generates the initial project files:

    .gitignore
    .python-version
    pyproject.toml
    README.md
    main.py

WHAT DOES EACH FILE DO?

.gitignore       - Specifies files and folders Git should ignore.
.python-version  - Specifies the project's Python version.
pyproject.toml   - Stores project configuration and dependencies.
README.md        - Contains project documentation.
main.py          - Contains the example Python script.

INITIAL PROJECT STRUCTURE:

my-awesome-project/
    .gitignore
    .python-version
    pyproject.toml
    README.md
    main.py

NOTE:

The .venv directory and uv.lock file are generally created when uv first needs to synchronize the project's dependencies.

For example, this happens when you run uv add, uv sync, or uv run.

STEP 4: OPEN THE PROJECT IN VS CODE

Enter the project directory:

    cd my-awesome-project

Open it in VS Code:

    code .

WHAT HAPPENS?

- VS Code opens with your project.
- You can edit your Python files.
- The Python extension can detect your project's virtual environment.
- You can run and debug your Python code directly from VS Code.

IMPORTANT:

Make sure the Python extension is installed in VS Code.

If the command code . does not work, configure the VS Code command-line launcher or open the project using VS Code's File > Open Folder option.

STEP 5: ADD PACKAGES

Open the integrated terminal in VS Code.

Shortcut:

    Ctrl + `

Add the packages required by your project:

    uv add requests
    uv add pandas numpy
    uv add python-dotenv

For interactive Python environments, you can also install:

    uv add --dev ipykernel

WHAT HAPPENS?

- Packages are downloaded and installed.
- pyproject.toml is updated with your dependencies.
- uv.lock records the resolved package versions.
- The project's .venv environment is created or synchronized.

STEP 6: TEST YOUR SETUP

Open main.py and replace its contents with the following code:

    import requests
    from dotenv import load_dotenv
    import os

    # Load environment variables
    load_dotenv()

    # Test the setup
    print("Packages imported successfully!")

    # Test environment variable
    api_key = os.environ.get("API_KEY", "not-set")

    if api_key != "not-set":
        print("API_KEY is configured successfully!")
    else:
        print("API_KEY is not configured.")

    # Test requests
    response = requests.get(
        "https://api.github.com",
        timeout=10
    )

    print(f"GitHub API status: {response.status_code}")

Run the application:

    uv run python main.py

EXPECTED RESULT:

    Packages imported successfully!
    API_KEY is not configured.
    GitHub API status: 200

The API key message will depend on whether you have configured your .env file.

The GitHub API status may vary depending on network connectivity or service availability.

STEP 7: SET UP ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES

Create a file named .env in your project directory.

Example .env file:

    API_KEY=your-secret-key-here
    DATABASE_URL=postgresql://localhost/mydb
    DEBUG=True

This file stores environment-specific configuration and sensitive values.

Now create another file named .env.example.

Example .env.example file:

    API_KEY=your-api-key-here
    DATABASE_URL=your-database-url
    DEBUG=True

WHY CREATE .env.example?

The .env file contains your actual configuration values.

The .env.example file provides a template for other developers so they know which environment variables they need to configure.

IMPORTANT SECURITY RULE:

Never commit your actual .env file to GitHub.

Open your .gitignore file and make sure it contains:

    .env
    .venv/
    __pycache__/
    *.py[cod]

The generated .gitignore may already contain some of these entries.

Check it before committing your project.

Also remember:

- Keep actual API keys private.
- Never hardcode secrets in your Python files.
- Do not share your .env file publicly.
- Commit .env.example instead of .env.

STEP 8: INITIALIZE GIT

Git helps you track changes in your project.

Open the VS Code terminal and run:

Initialize the Git repository:

    git init

Stage your project files:

    git add .

Create your first commit:

    git commit -m "Initial commit"

WHAT HAPPENS?

- Git starts tracking your project.
- Files that are not excluded by .gitignore are staged.
- Your first project snapshot is saved as a commit.

NOTE:

Make sure .env is listed in .gitignore before running git add .

If you have already staged a sensitive file, remove it from the staging area before committing.

STEP 9: CREATE A GITHUB REPOSITORY

GitHub allows you to store your Git repository online and collaborate with other developers.

You can create a GitHub repository directly from your terminal using GitHub CLI.

USING GITHUB CLI (RECOMMENDED)

Create a private repository:

    gh repo create my-awesome-project --private --source=. --remote=origin --push

Or create a public repository:

    gh repo create my-awesome-project --public --source=. --remote=origin --push

WHAT HAPPENS?

- A repository is created on GitHub.
- Your local repository is connected to GitHub.
- The remote is configured with the name origin.
- Your committed code is pushed to GitHub.

INSTALLING GITHUB CLI

If GitHub CLI is not installed, install it using the instructions for your operating system.

macOS (Homebrew):

    brew install gh

Windows (winget):

    winget install --id GitHub.cli

You can also download it from:

    https://cli.github.com/

Linux (Debian/Ubuntu):

Follow the official GitHub CLI installation instructions:

    https://github.com/cli/cli/blob/trunk/docs/install_linux.md

After installing GitHub CLI, authenticate with GitHub:

    gh auth login

Follow the instructions displayed in your terminal.

IMPORTANT:

The GitHub CLI command above assumes that you have already initialized Git and created at least one commit.

